{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 40, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "ועלינו", :translit "ve'alinu", :gloss "and we will go up"} {:he "הננו ועלינו", :translit "hinenu ve'alinu", :gloss "here we are and we will go up"} {:he "ואם כה יאמרו עלו עלינו", :translit "ve'im koh yomru alu alenu", :gloss "and if thus they say: go up, we will go up"} {:he "ועלינו", :translit "ve'alinu", :gloss "and we will go up"} {:he "כי אין לה' מעצור להושיע ברב או במעט", :translit "ki ein la-Shem ma'atzor lehoshia berov o b'mi'at", :gloss "for there is no restraint to YHWH to save by many or by few"} {:he "ששים רבוא", :translit "shishim ribo", :gloss "sixty myriads"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "The word ve'alinu appears multiple times in Numbers 14:40, flagged by the Masoretic note 'bet' (two occurrences); the same form recurs in 1 Samuel 14:6 and 1 Samuel 14:7, linking the passages thematically."}], :frame {:text "Numbers 14:40 records the people declaring 'we will go up' after God's decree of wandering, but their attempt fails. Jonathan makes the same declaration of ascent ('alu alenu') in 1 Samuel 14:6–7, attacking the Philistines with only his armor-bearer, yet prevailing. The verse 'For there is no restraint to YHWH to save by many or by few' is Jonathan's own words in 1 Samuel 14:6. Ba'al HaTurim contrasts the two outcomes: success depends on divine sanction, not numerical strength (1 Samuel 14:6).", :sources ["1 Samuel 14:6" "1 Samuel 14:7" "Numbers 14:40–45"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "The word ve'alinu appears multiple times in Numbers 14:40, flagged by the Masoretic note 'bet' (two occurrences); the same form recurs in 1 Samuel 14:6 and 1 Samuel 14:7, linking the passages thematically.", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :numbers, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:29:08.082497373Z", :model "glm-5.2"}